  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reducing the Illegal Wildlife Trade Across Southern Lake Tanganyika</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lake Tanganyika is located in northern Zambia and is the second largest freshwater lake in the world. It is home to an immense amount of biodiversity including hundreds of cichlid species. Many of the cichlid species are in high demand for the worldwide aquarium trade. In the first quarter of 2018, half of all illegal incidents in Nsumbu National Park (one of only three protected areas around Lake Tanganyika) were aquatic in nature. To address the illegal harvesting of protected cichlid species in Nsumbu National Park, this one-year grant will support: recruitment and training of rangers at Chunga Wildlife College, a requirement for rangers in Zambia; boat operation and aquatic training for existing rangers, including how to identify species and trade routes of cichlids to better direct law enforcement operations; workshops with relevant national wildlife and fisheries authorities on methods of live fish collection, international export routes, and value of fish exports; provision of fuel, rations, and boat engine maintenance to ensure continuity of aquatic patrols of Lake Tanganyika; and integration of the aquatic patrols into the wider law enforcement strategy for Nsumbu National Park to ensure intelligence and investigations are streamlined.</narrative>
  </description>
